{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:47:31.712Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"34","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"CONFIRMED and FIXED in feb63a86. The premise is exactly right, including the mechanism.

REPRODUCED FIRST, before touching anything. Input = one resolvable diagnostic, one 5 MiB line,
one more resolvable diagnostic:

    build := "./a.go:1:1: undefined: Alpha\n" + strings.Repeat("x", 5*1024*1024) + "\n./b.go:2:2: undefined: Beta\n"
    candidates, scanned, err := explainCandidates(strings.NewReader(build), 8)

    candidates=[Alpha] scanned=1 err=bufio.Scanner: token too long

Beta is gone. bufio.Scanner returns ErrTooLong at that token and Scan() then returns false
forever, so everything after the long line was never looked at. The drain that follows
(io.Copy(io.Discard, source)) does keep the echo whole -- the agent still SEES those lines --
which is precisely what made this quiet: the output looked complete while explain resolved
nothing from half of it. Tolerating ErrTooLong at explain.go:241 stopped the command failing; it
did not restore the scan.

FIX: read lines from a bufio.Reader rather than a Scanner. New helper explainReadLine appends
at most explainMaxLineBytes (4 MiB, the ceiling the Scanner was already configured with, so no
line that used to be scanned is scanned less) and discards the remainder of an over-long line by
looping on bufio.ErrBufferFull, so the next call starts on the NEXT line. ErrTooLong can no
longer occur, so the special case at the call site is gone rather than left as a decoy.

REGRESSION TESTS, both in internal/cli/explain_context_test.go. On a compile-clean revert --
explainCandidates put back on bufio.NewScanner with the same buffer bounds and the ErrTooLong
tolerance restored; go build ./... and go vet ./internal/cli/ both clean, "REVERT COMPILES
CLEAN" -- both fail:

    TestExplainKeepsScanningPastAnOverLongLine
      explain_context_test.go:252: names = [Alpha], want [Alpha Beta]: the diagnostic after the over-long line was dropped

    TestExplainTruncatesAnOverLongLineRatherThanJoiningIt
      explain_context_test.go:276: candidates = [], want only Gamma: text past the per-line cap is not scanned, and the next line must still be

The second one is the guard on the fix rather than on the bug: it plants a name PAST the per-line
cap and a different name on the following line, and pins that the capped text is not scanned, the
following line still is, and the long line's bytes do not leak into the next line's file context
(it asserts Gamma resolves with file c.go).

MEMORY UNCHANGED, which was the property the Scanner's buffer bound existed to protect and the
one a naive "just read the whole line" fix would have destroyed. Measured with runtime.MemStats
across explainCandidates on a 300 MB single line sandwiched between two diagnostics:

    candidates=2 err=<nil>  heapAllocDelta=8 MB  totalAlloc=23 MB

Both diagnostics resolved, 8 MB of heap for a 300 MB line. ReadSlice never allocates past the
reader's own 64 KiB buffer, and at most 4 MiB of any single line is retained.

VERIFICATION: go build ./... clean; go vet ./internal/cli/ clean; gofmt clean; go test
./internal/cli/ -count=1 passes in full (36.2s), which includes the two tests added earlier this
round for the --max-symbols budget.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1S6VZV1HS0F1TFSCPGK4HQR","targetId":"01M1S6VZVEXZFP4XETAS2WKSX7","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NGN3YVFB3SMZCH1CBV49WV"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
