{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:55:41.998Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"39","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"DUPLICATE of 01M1P6VKT20RH850K1JE3JRSRB, same anchor (internal/cli/help.go:250), dismissed
earlier this round with measurements. Routed to design issue #215
(https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/issues/215), which is open. Re-posting it does not
change the disposition, but this version adds two things, so both are answered here and appended
to #215 rather than dropped.

WHAT WAS ALREADY SETTLED, briefly, so nobody re-derives it:

  "defeating the new streaming behavior" -- REFUTED, measured. explain's own peak RSS is
  217,759,744 B at 10 MB of input and 219,856,896 B at 100 MB: +2.1 MB for +90 MB. The streaming
  fix does exactly what it was written to do. The buffer is in the SHELL, and it did not come
  back into explain.

  "can again consume unbounded memory" -- CONFIRMED and larger than stated. Same 100 MB:
  `cat f | cat` peaks at 2,146,304 B, `o=$(cat f); printf '%s\n' "$o"` at 604,733,440 B. 281x,
  scaling with output size.

  Not fixed here because the plain-pipe revert reopens the defect this PR closed -- verified,
  `/bin/sh -c "false 2>&1 | entire graph explain ..."` exits 0, the capture exits 1 -- and
  because the shape is a documented product surface at four sites, one of which (the emitted
  VERIFY: line, `prefix = "( o=$("` in internal/sem/search_verify.go) is on the unmerged branch
  fix/verify-exit-codes-and-injection. Changing help.go alone would make three shapes where
  there are two.

NEW IN THIS VERSION, and both accepted:

1. "emits no incremental output" -- CONFIRMED, and it is the strongest argument in the finding.
   Timestamps of lines arriving at the far end, with 3 seconds between the two lines produced:

     plain pipe    1788627290.195 first     capture     1788627296.208 first
                   1788627293.196 second                1788627296.211 second

   3.0s apart as produced, versus 3ms apart all at the end. On a hung build that is the
   difference between watching it fail and waiting blind. Added to #215.

2. The suggested remedy -- "a temporary status file or an explicit portable wrapper" -- is the
   right direction, and #215 already carries a verified instance of the second option that is
   strictly better than the first:

     ( exec 4>&1; r=$( { { <cmd> 2>&1; echo $? >&3; } | entire graph explain --repo . >&4; } 3>&1 ); exit $r )

   Verified under dash: exit status preserved (rc=7 through the pipeline), 1,523,712 B peak on
   the same 100 MB input, 397x below the capture form, and it streams. The status-file shape also
   works but pays for it with a writable TMPDIR, cleanup on signals, and an mktemp failure mode
   the fd form does not have. Both are now written up in #215.

Note for whoever reads this trail: help.go:250 has now been raised twice and internal/cli/explain.go
twice, in four consecutive review rounds. The two explain.go findings were real and are fixed
(0cc4f28a, feb63a86, each with a regression test that fails on a compile-clean revert). This one
is the same finding a second time against an unchanged line whose disposition is a maintainer
decision. Flagging as thrash and stopping the loop here rather than re-triggering the gate again.","to":"dismissed"},"reviewId":"01M1S7HHDVQF3P0P86PWGTSTEK","targetId":"01M1S7HHEFBYQ4FTG71EGXQ7NW","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NGN3YVFB3SMZCH1CBV49WV"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
